WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:05.003
A key flashes on a screen.

2
00:00:05.903 --> 00:00:07.143
The man says he saw nothing.

3
00:00:08.083 --> 00:00:09.663
Yet his left hand picks out the key.

4
00:00:10.583 --> 00:00:11.563
How?

5
00:00:11.833 --> 00:00:16.833
Cutting the forebrain commissures was used to treat epilepsy that drugs could not

6
00:00:16.833 --> 00:00:20.593
control. The corpus callosum is the largest commissure.

7
00:00:21.567 --> 00:00:27.307
In 1962, Gazzaniga, Bogen and Sperry reported one such patient, W.J.

8
00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:32.010
Blindfolded, he could use familiar objects with his left hand.

9
00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:33.950
But he could not name them.

10
00:00:34.883 --> 00:00:36.083
First, the crossings.

11
00:00:37.103 --> 00:00:41.563
At the optic chiasm, only the fibres from the nasal half of each retina cross.

12
00:00:42.633 --> 00:00:47.273
So everything left of the point he stares at reaches the right hemisphere, from both

13
00:00:47.273 --> 00:00:50.213
eyes. The hemifield matters, not the eye.

14
00:00:51.183 --> 00:00:54.323
A brief flash reduces the chance that he looks toward it.

15
00:00:55.003 --> 00:00:59.523
We still monitor his gaze and discard trials with an eye movement, which could

16
00:00:59.523 --> 00:01:01.463
expose the picture to the other hemisphere.

17
00:01:02.566 --> 00:01:05.466
Touch from the left hand reaches the right hemisphere.

18
00:01:05.466 --> 00:01:09.426
And each hand is controlled mainly by the opposite hemisphere.

19
00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:11.590
Now apply the rule.

20
00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:14.910
You can say only what reaches the speaking hemisphere.

21
00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:17.410
In most people, that is the left.

22
00:01:18.433 --> 00:01:21.513
The key in the left field reaches only the right hemisphere.

23
00:01:22.433 --> 00:01:24.473
With the bridge cut, it has no route to speech.

24
00:01:25.566 --> 00:01:28.106
So he cannot name it, and says he saw nothing.

25
00:01:28.926 --> 00:01:33.226
But his right hemisphere feels with and controls the left hand, which finds the key.

26
00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:38.070
Now his eyes are closed, and a spoon is placed in his right hand.

27
00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:39.870
Can he name it?

28
00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:51.020
Yes. Touch from the right hand crosses in the medulla to the left hemisphere, which

29
00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:52.020
usually speaks.

30
00:01:52.833 --> 00:01:55.833
speech were on the right, the naming answers would flip.

31
00:01:56.653 --> 00:01:58.553
The hands would still follow the same crossings.

32
00:01:59.683 --> 00:02:03.703
Still, the disconnected right hemisphere understands some spoken words.

33
00:02:04.623 --> 00:02:06.283
It is mute more often than it is deaf.

34
00:02:07.316 --> 00:02:08.536
That fits the route.

35
00:02:09.456 --> 00:02:14.196
Understanding words gets help from both sides, but the road to speech usually runs

36
00:02:14.196 --> 00:02:15.196
on the left.

37
00:02:15.766 --> 00:02:18.686
The tidy rule has exceptions in two later patients.

38
00:02:19.606 --> 00:02:20.906
That debate is in the lesson.

39
00:02:21.916 --> 00:02:25.716
His right hemisphere can still see, feel and understand some words.

40
00:02:26.576 --> 00:02:29.356
But he cannot say what never reaches the speaking hemisphere.
